  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <Faq faqs={[
      { question: "Why are my cannabis seedlings stretching toward the light?", answer: "Insufficient light intensity. Cannabis seedlings need 18-24 hours of direct light from a T5 fluorescent or LED seed-starting fixture mounted 4-6 inches above the canopy. A windowsill in Maine's spring light is rarely enough — seedlings stretch toward the brightest source, which may not be intense enough to prevent leggy growth." },
      { question: "What does damping off look like on cannabis seedlings?", answer: "A seedling wilts suddenly even though the soil is moist. The stem at the soil line is brown, soft, and pinched. The fungus (Pythium, Rhizoctonia, or Fusarium) thrives in cold wet conditions. Sterilized seedling mix, 65-75°F soil, and gentle airflow are the best prevention." },
      { question: "Can I save a yellowing cannabis seedling?", answer: "Often yes, if true leaves are still green. Yellow cotyledons are normal — the plant is using stored energy and they will drop on their own. Yellow true leaves indicate nutrient or watering issues that are usually fixable with adjusted feeding or a watering schedule of 'when the top inch is dry'." },
      { question: "When can I transplant my Maine cannabis seedlings outdoors?", answer: "Southern Maine: late May after last frost (May 5-15 average). Central Maine: early June after last frost (May 15-25 average). Northern/Aroostook: mid-June (June 5-15 average). Check the UMaine Cooperative Extension frost-date guidance for your specific town — microclimate shifts of 7-14 days are common." },
      { question: "Do cannabis seedlings need nutrients?", answer: "No for the first 2-3 weeks. The cotyledons (seed leaves) and seed energy reserves provide all nutrition until 3-4 true leaves develop. Starting nutrients earlier than that causes nutrient burn in most cases. Use plain pH-balanced water for the first 2 weeks of seedling growth." },
      { question: "What humidity do cannabis seedlings need in Maine?", answer: "65-75% RH for the first 2 weeks, dropping to 55-65% by week 3-4. Maine's indoor spring humidity typically runs 40-60% by April-May, so you may need a humidity dome for the first 1-2 weeks. Don't exceed 80% — that creates the conditions for damping off and mold." }
    ]} />
  </section>

